 ## Overview
-Single and multiple scattering methods simulate the interaction of light with participating media (such as fog, smoke, or clouds) by modeling how photons are absorbed, emitted, and redirected by particles within the volume. While single scattering approximations assume light undergoes at most one collision before reaching the camera, multiple scattering methods account for complex, multi-bounce light transport, enabling the rendering of dense, glowing, or highly reflective volumetric effects. This family is distinguished from [Optical models](33-optical-models.md) by its focus on the stochastic or deterministic solution of the Radiative Transfer Equation (RTE) for light paths, and from [Local illumination, shadows and ambient occlusion](34-local-illumination-shadows-and-ambient-occlusion.md) by its explicit handling of in-scattering from distant light sources rather than just local shading.
